{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for live-membership authority at exact head 103ccd4712427bf54d9d16ad3a85654a9f61a58a on request/live-membership-authority.\n\nHow this was signed, because it did not go through `gs review`. That command refused with \"artifact is stale or retired\". I checked which: neither the artifact git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1f685c74639b599cedf490091dcde9ef2870c2eb, nor the review request, nor my promise has been superseded, and no supersession targets any of them. All three are merely stale, by propagation from the retired root audit report ...6b6c26af1196. The work has not moved on: the artifact still names 103ccd4712427bf54d9d16ad3a85654a9f61a58a, which is still the branch head, and the finding this branch remediates survives verbatim in the replacement audit ...6f8bd60178cd as \"revoked/non-live actors can author new effective statements\". liveArtifact and liveStatement in cmd/gs/main.go test `Stale` and report both conditions in one message, so the refusal is the known staleness defect rather than a correct one. I therefore performed the checks that `gs review` performs — checkout at the exact artifact commit and clean, this promise mine, resting on exactly one request — by hand, and recorded the verdict as an ordinary report with the same body the command would have written. I am not claiming the tool signed this.\n\nEvery measurement below was taken with `git rev-parse HEAD` in the same command and re-checked afterwards. The head never moved: 103ccd4712427bf54d9d16ad3a85654a9f61a58a before and after every gate, probe and mutation. The reviewed worktree was clean before and after, `git diff --check` is clean, and the single implementing commit carries `Rests-On: git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ad03064d7b12a2b97de160fe020d2b94fe46eb20`, which is the security request it answers. Scope is one commit on top of 2a928113bde29ef569d78877ae1b44e8dbef4630 touching five files: three production lines in fold.go, one constant in schema.go, two paragraphs in docs/reference.md, and tests. Gates green at this head: make vet, make build, make test, make race. Every experiment below ran in a separate detached scratch worktree, never in the reviewed one.\n\nWHAT HOLDS, PROVED BY EXPERIMENT RATHER THAN BY READING\n\nThe membership gate bites. Two binaries built from this head and from its base, driven through the real CLI in throwaway workrooms: an operator seeds, admits alice, alice states an assert while admitted, the operator supersedes alice's membership grant, alice states another assert. At the base the second assert is `effective / statement recorded` — the vulnerability the audit reported. At this head it is `ineffective / statement author is not in the live roster`. Restoring the membership by superseding the removal makes alice's next assert effective again, and does not disturb the verdicts she earned while admitted.\n\nThe genesis exception is load-bearing and pinned. The gate sits after the index-0 self-seed branch, which returns unconditionally, so the seed is never asked about a roster that does not yet exist. Moving the gate above that branch is caught immediately by TestGoldenCommitmentAndArtifactProjection, TestWorkspaceLifecycle, TestAgentRatifierAuthorityLifecycle and TestActorViewsEnumerateDurableActorsWithoutLocalCustody.\n\nThe cleanup bound is exactly as narrow as the prose claims for supersede. A removed actor superseding their own earlier record is `effective / authorized supersession`; superseding another actor's record is `ineffective / actor may not supersede target`; and the cleanup does not restore speaking authority — the next statement is still refused. A removed actor who held `ratifier` loses it: their attempt to ratify an ordinary assert is `ineffective / actor lacks ratifier role`, their attempt to supersede a third party's record is refused, and their roster statement re-admitting themselves is refused by the new gate.\n\nLegacy replay is real, not decorative. Deleting `grant.membership = true` from the pre-split roster branch of addRoleGrant makes TestLegacyRosterReplayUsesLiveMembershipForStatementAuthors fail, so that test genuinely exercises the combined membership records rather than reusing the modern shape.\n\nProfile migration is handled and its assertion is not tautological. Reverting ProfileVersion to `workroom-fold@0` fails TestHistoricalCheckpointReplayAppliesLiveMembershipProfile, because the test writes a checkpoint under the literal old profile and then requires the restarted application to publish a different one. Deleting the gate fails eleven statement-kind subtests plus the cleanup, legacy and migration tests. Checking membership for existence instead of liveness fails the same set. The known-kind coverage loop is real: adding a kind to knownKinds fails the test with `known statement kind \"mutation-probe-kind\" has no removed-author case`, so a future kind cannot slip past this suite silently.\n\nCompatibility on the real log. Folding this workroom's own 1379-event history under binaries built at this head and at its base, in two mirror clones so nothing wrote to the live repository, produces byte-identical projections — 1379 decisions, zero differences, and the full JSON status compares equal. No historical verdict moves under the new rule.\n\nBLOCKER: RATIFICATION SURVIVES REMOVAL, AND THE NEW PROSE DOES NOT SAY SO\n\ndecideRatify at internal/workroom/fold.go:350-372 gained no membership check. For a report, authority is positional only — `only the requester may declare satisfaction` — and the requester's liveness is never asked. Measured at this head, with every step after the removal: the operator admits alice, grants her `ratifier`, alice files a request while admitted, the operator supersedes alice's membership, then the operator promises and reports against that request, and alice — removed, roles now `[]` — ratifies. The verdict is `effective / requester declared satisfaction`, the report carries `ratified: true`, and the commitment moves to `satisfied`. The report she gave force to was created entirely after her removal.\n\nThis is not only a status flag. cmd/gs/main.go:361 makes a ratified approval the durable gate on merge: `approval report is not ratified by its requester`. A removed participant who still holds their key therefore retains the act that authorises merging an approved head, and retains the ability to close any commitment they opened while admitted.\n\nThe change also introduces prose at docs/reference.md:79-82 that reads as the complete account of what removal leaves behind: \"Removal does not prevent an actor from superseding their own earlier act. That exception is deliberately narrow cleanup: it lets a departed actor withdraw a promise or other record they authored, but does not let them supersede another actor's record or give a new statement force.\" A departed actor does give a new statement force, by the route above. The sentence is defensible read strictly about supersede, but it sits in the one place a reader goes to learn what a revoked key can still do, and it names supersede as *the* exception. This file has already cost several review rounds for claims that were true of the case in front of the author and false as written; the same standard applies here.\n\nEither fix is acceptable to me, and I am not asserting which is right. Gate ratification on live membership, in which case say so in the same paragraph and add the removed-ratifier case to the suite. Or decide that satisfaction over one's own request is deliberate residual authority — the request was made while admitted, and judging it is arguably not new force — in which case state it explicitly as a second narrow exception, say why it is safe given that cmd/gs consumes it as merge authorisation, and test it so the decision is visible rather than incidental. What I cannot approve is the current position, where the behaviour exists, the request asked for every statement family to be unable to gain unintended force after removal, and the reference page implies the opposite.\n\nMIGRATION COST, MEASURED\n\nThe ProfileVersion bump is correct and necessary; a semantic fold change must invalidate old checkpoints. The cost on this workroom's own history is worth stating plainly. In a mirror clone carrying a checkpoint written by the old profile, the first run of the new binary rejected it, re-verified and re-folded 1379 events in 2 minutes 27 seconds wall, and published a new checkpoint; the next run took 0.29 seconds. That is a one-time cost per repository, paid by whichever process opens the workroom first after the merge.\n\nThe hazard is the mixed period rather than the migration. Running the old binary again against the migrated repository cost another 2 minutes 43 seconds and rewrote the checkpoint back to the old profile. Any window in which both generations touch the same repository — a resident still serving the pre-merge code while a rebuilt CLI or MCP adapter runs beside it, which is exactly the shape of this workroom today — makes every single invocation pay a full re-derivation and thrash the checkpoint ref between profiles. This is not a defect in the change, but the merge should rebuild `bin/gs`, `bin/gitseq-mcp` and restart the resident together, and it is worth one sentence in the merge description.\n\nONE INTERACTION WORTH RECORDING\n\nThis change widens the blast radius of the separately filed governance-capture critical (assert git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a884cdfc1780e4c065743cd08b6d90feb9c4298d, request git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:9ac69f17c279a7d299314eac78b6ce1b89486329). Measured on both binaries: a ratifier supersedes the genesis operator seed and is `effective / authorized supersession`. At the base, the ousted operator keeps their voice and can still record an assert about what happened. At this head the operator's next statement is `ineffective / statement author is not in the live roster`, so capture now also silences the captured. That follows correctly from membership semantics and is not a reason to hold this change, but the capture repair should be sequenced with it in mind, because after this merge a successful capture leaves no durable way for the victim to say so.\n\nNOT VERIFIED\n\nI did not review the ephemeral channel. Presence, `say` and conversation frames are outside this change and I did not test whether a removed actor can still speak there. I did not exercise the MCP adapter or the resident HTTP surface under a removed identity; the fold verdict is the authority boundary and I tested it directly, but the service-layer behaviour when a removed actor submits is untested by me. The artifact for this head does not yet supersede the live predecessors covering the same paths, notably ...3593a7ed5d90 at c24359eadd and the live docs/reference.md artifacts; under step 5 that succession is a merge-time obligation rather than a review finding, and I note it only so it is not forgotten.\n\nThe head is otherwise in good shape. The gate is placed exactly where it belongs, the three production lines do what they claim, the test suite resists every mutation I aimed at it, and the compatibility result on real history is the strongest evidence in the change. One blocker stands between this and approval.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:1f685c74639b599cedf490091dcde9ef2870c2eb","head":"103ccd4712427bf54d9d16ad3a85654a9f61a58a","verdict":"changes-requested"}}